{"name":"Liver Brown","hex":"#513e32","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"liver","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A large organ in the body that stores and metabolizes nutrients, destroys toxins and produces bile. It is responsible for thousands of biochemical reactions.","This organ, as taken from animals used as food."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of the colour of liver (dark brown, tinted with red and gray)."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/liver"},{"term":"brown","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour like that of chocolate or coffee.","One of the colour balls used in snooker, with a value of 4 points."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a brown color/colour.","Gloomy."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/brown"}],"entries":[{"title":"Liver","blocks":[{"text":"The liver is a major metabolic organ exclusively found in vertebrates which performs many essential biological functions, such as detoxification of the organism and the synthesis of various proteins and other biochemicals necessary for digestion and growth. In humans, it is located in the right upper quadrant of the abdomen, below the diaphragm and mostly shielded by the lower right rib cage. Its other metabolic roles include carbohydrate metabolism, the production of a number of hormones, conversion and storage of nutrients such as glucose and glycogen, and the decomposition of red blood cells. Anatomical and medical terminology often use the prefix hepat- from ἡπατο-, from the Greek word for liver, such as hepatology, and hepatitis."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Anatomy_Abdomen_Tiesworks.jpg/330px-Anatomy_Abdomen_Tiesworks.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liver","wikidata":"Q9368"},{"title":"Brown","blocks":[{"text":"Brown is a color. It can be thought as a darker, typically desaturated shade of orange, and can often be produced by combining red and yellow with another color, namely blue."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg/330px-Espresso-roasted_coffee_beans.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Brown","wikidata":"Q47071"},{"title":"Liver (color)","blocks":[{"text":"Liver is a color name for a kind of brown, first recorded as such in English in 1686. It may refer to the color of the organ."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liver_(color)","wikidata":"Q536284"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Liver_01_animation1.gif/330px-Liver_01_animation1.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/88/Liver_01_animation1.gif/330px-Liver_01_animation1.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALiver_01_animation1.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Gray1085.png/500px-Gray1085.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Gray1085.png/500px-Gray1085.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The liver, viewed from above, showing the left and right lobes separated by the falciform ligament","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGray1085.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Sobo_1906_389.png/500px-Sobo_1906_389.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/cc/Sobo_1906_389.png/1280px-Sobo_1906_389.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.or